---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';
const w = (id: string) => url(`w/${id}/`);
const g = (id: string) => url(`glossary/#${id}`);
---
<Base title="Reading a UBS apparatus entry" nav="guide/apparatus/" description="A worked example from John 1:18, explained piece by piece.">
  <article class="prose guide">
    <p class="label">Guide</p>
    <h1>How to read a UBS apparatus entry</h1>
    <p class="lead">An apparatus entry lists the different readings at one place in the text, and the witnesses for each. Here is the entry for John 1:18 as Paratext shows it, taken apart.</p>

    <h2>The verse</h2>
    <p>John 1:18 says that no one has ever seen God, but someone close to the Father has made him known. The manuscripts disagree on who that someone is called.</p>

    <h2>The entry</h2>
    <p class="small muted">Shortened here: the third reading has many more witnesses than shown. The marks ①–⑥ point to the notes below.</p>
    <div class="entry" role="figure" aria-label="UBS5 apparatus entry for John 1:18, shortened">
      <p><b>1.18</b> <span class="mk">①</span><span class="sig">{'{B}'}</span> <span lang="grc">μονογενὴς θεός</span> <span class="mk">②</span><span class="sig">𝔓⁶⁶ <span lang="he">א</span>* B C* L</span> syr<sup>p, hmg</sup> geo² <span class="mk">③</span>Origen<sup>gr 2/4</sup> Didymus Cyril<sup>1/4</sup></p>
      <p><span class="mk">④</span><b>//</b> <span lang="grc">ὁ μονογενὴς θεός</span> <span class="sig">𝔓⁷⁵ <span lang="he">א</span>‎²</span> 33 cop<sup>bo</sup> Clement<sup>2/3</sup> … Cyril<sup>2/4</sup></p>
      <p><b>//</b> <span lang="grc">ὁ μονογενὴς υἱός</span> <span class="sig">A C³ W<sup>supp</sup> Δ Θ Ψ</span> … <span class="mk">⑤</span><span class="sig">Byz [E F G H] Lect</span> it<sup>a, aur, b…</sup> vg syr<sup>c, h, pal</sup> … <span class="mk">⑥</span>REB BJ</p>
      <p><b>//</b> <span lang="grc">μονογενὴς υἱὸς θεοῦ</span> it<sup>q</sup> Irenaeus<sup>lat 1/3</sup>; Ambrose<sup>1/11 vid</sup></p>
      <p><b>//</b> <span lang="grc">ὁ μονογενής</span> vg<sup>ms</sup> Ps-Vigilius<sup>1/2</sup></p>
    </div>
    <p class="small muted">Source: UBS Greek New Testament, 5th ed., apparatus at John 1:18, as shown in Paratext 9 (seen 19 Nov 2026). Quoted in part for teaching.</p>

    <h2>Piece by piece</h2>
    <ol class="notes">
      <li><strong>The rating, <a href={g('rating')}>{'{B}'}</a>.</strong> The committee's letter for how sure it was of the reading printed in the text. It comes first, before the readings. It is an opinion, not a measure of the evidence: see <a href="#ratings">what the ratings do and do not tell you</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>The first reading is the one printed in the text.</strong> Then come its witnesses, always in the same order: Greek manuscripts (papyri, then majuscules, then minuscules), then ancient versions, then church fathers. Here: <a href={w('p66')}>𝔓⁶⁶</a>, the first hand of <a href={w('sinaiticus')}><span lang="he">א</span>*</a>, <a href={w('vaticanus')}>B</a>, the first hand of C, and L. Then the Peshitta Syriac and the margin of the Harclean Syriac, and the second Georgian revision.</li>
      <li><strong>Church fathers carry small marks.</strong> Origen<sup>gr 2/4</sup>: Origen, in his works that survive in Greek (<a href={g('gr-lat')}>gr</a>), quotes the verse four times; two of those quotations support this reading (<a href={g('fraction')}>2/4</a>).</li>
      <li><strong><a href={g('double-slash')}>//</a> starts the next reading.</strong> The second reading adds the article ὁ. Its witnesses include <span lang="he">א</span>‎², a later <a href={g('correctors')}>corrector</a> of Sinaiticus. So Sinaiticus appears twice in one entry: what the first scribe wrote, and what a corrector changed it to.</li>
      <li><strong>Group sigla.</strong> <a href={w('byz')}>Byz</a> is the majority of Byzantine manuscripts; the letters in square brackets name some of them (<a href={g('byz-brackets')}>[ ]</a>). <a href={g('lect')}>Lect</a> is the majority of the lectionaries. W<sup>supp</sup> is a part of Codex W <a href={g('supp')}>supplied by a later hand</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Modern translations.</strong> UBS5 also names some modern translations that follow a reading. Here REB (Revised English Bible) and BJ (Bible de Jérusalem) follow "the only Son".</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What a translator does with this</h2>
    <p>Do not count witnesses. The third reading has the most manuscripts by far, but most of them are Byzantine copies made centuries later. Look at how early the witnesses are and how widely spread: the first reading has early Greek witnesses (𝔓⁶⁶ was copied around 200), while the third reading is spread across Greek, Latin and Syriac and early fathers.</p>
    <p>Read the readings as a story of change, and ask which reading best explains how the others arose. Scholars answer that question differently here:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>For "God" (the UBS text).</strong> The committee's reasons, as Metzger reports them:<sup class="cite"><a href="#src-metzger">1</a></sup> the papyri 𝔓⁶⁶ and 𝔓⁷⁵, both reading θεός, made its external support much stronger; "Son" is the easier reading and probably came from scribes making the verse match John 3:16, 18 and 1 John 4:9; and θεός without the article, as in 1:1, looks older, since no scribe had a reason to remove the article, while anyone changing to υἱός would add it.</li>
      <li><strong>For "Son".</strong> It is found in every region and language, and in 2nd- and 3rd-century fathers, while "God" is found almost only in Egyptian ("Alexandrian") witnesses. "Son" fits how John writes elsewhere. And a scribe defending Christ's full deity had a reason to change "Son" to "God". Bart Ehrman argues this view (<i>The Orthodox Corruption of Scripture</i>, ch. 2, on John 1:18).</li>
      <li><strong>The shortest reading, ὁ μονογενής.</strong> The committee found it attractive on internal grounds but too weakly attested to print.<sup class="cite"><a href="#src-metzger">1</a></sup></li>
    </ul>
    <p>The decision was by a majority, not unanimous. One committee member (initials A.W.) added a dissenting note: he doubted John wrote μονογενὴς θεός, suggested it may be an early copying slip in the Alexandrian tradition (ΥΣ read as ΘΣ, see <a href={url('background/materials/')}>nomina sacra</a>), and said at least a {'{D}'} rating would be better.<sup class="cite"><a href="#src-metzger">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>So the <a href="#ratings">{'{B}'}</a> rating hides a real disagreement inside the committee as well as outside it. Then check how your reference translations handle it. Many English translations print "God" or "the only God" and give "Son" in a footnote; REB and BJ do the opposite.</p>
    <p class="small muted">Metzger also notes that some commentators read μονογενής as a noun, giving three separate titles: "the only one, God, who is at the Father's side".<sup class="cite"><a href="#src-metzger">1</a></sup></p>
    <ol class="small srcs"><li id="src-metzger">Bruce M. Metzger, <i>A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament</i>, 2nd ed. (United Bible Societies, 1994), pp. 169–70, on John 1:18. Written for UBS4, which gave the same {'{B}'} rating as UBS5.</li></ol>

    <section class="unchecked-box" aria-labelledby="ratings">
      <h2 id="ratings">What the ratings do and do not tell you <span class="tag">not yet checked</span></h2>
      <p class="small muted">This section is written from memory of the handbooks and has not been checked against them yet.</p>
      <p>The rating is the committee's judgement, not a measure of the evidence. {'{A}'} means the committee was certain; {'{D}'} means it had great difficulty deciding. The letters do not count manuscripts, and two {'{B}'} ratings in different books were not made on a common scale.</p>
      <p>Scholars have criticised the system. Between the 3rd and 4th editions many ratings were raised, for example from {'{C}'} to {'{B}'}, without new evidence. Kent D. Clarke (<i>Textual Optimism</i>, 1997) argued that the ratings became more confident than the evidence allows. Treat the letter as one opinion, and read the reasons in Metzger's <i>Textual Commentary</i> or Omanson's <i>A Textual Guide to the Greek New Testament</i>.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
<style>
  .guide { margin-top: 1.5rem; }
  .lead { font-size: var(--step-1); line-height: 1.45; }
  .entry { background: var(--paper-2); border-radius: 6px; padding: 0.75rem 1rem; overflow-x: auto; font-family: var(--sigla); line-height: 1.9; }
  .entry p { margin: 0 0 0.35rem; }
  .entry sup { font-size: 0.7em; }
  .sig { color: var(--rubric); }
  .mk { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.8em; color: var(--ink-2); margin-right: 0.15em; }
  .notes { padding-left: 1.4rem; }
  .notes li { margin-bottom: 0.8rem; }
  .srcs { padding-left: 1.4rem; color: var(--ink-2); }
  .srcs li:target { background: var(--paper-2); }
  [lang="grc"] { font-family: var(--serif); }
  .unchecked-box { border: 1px dashed var(--rule); border-radius: 6px; padding: 0 1rem 0.25rem; margin-top: 2.5rem; }
  .unchecked-box h2 { margin-top: 1rem; }
</style>
